WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.259
Local AI performance depends on the machine, the model, and the configuration.

2
00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:16.639
Available RAM, accelerator memory where present, CPU speed, storage speed, model size, context length, and serving settings all influence response quality and latency.

3
00:00:16.639 --> 00:00:21.113
A workflow that feels smooth for one task may struggle with another.

4
00:00:21.113 --> 00:00:23.310
Reliability needs observation.

5
00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:32.885
Watch for memory pressure, slow responses, model loading time, failed requests, truncated context, repeated restarts, and user frustration.

6
00:00:32.885 --> 00:00:38.693
These signals show whether the workflow is still experimental or ready for heavier use.

7
00:00:38.693 --> 00:00:47.405
Important prompts, configuration notes, evaluation records, and workflow documentation should be backed up where appropriate.

8
00:00:47.405 --> 00:00:50.074
The goal is not to save every casual chat.

9
00:00:50.074 --> 00:00:55.960
The goal is to preserve the records needed to rebuild or understand important workflows.

10
00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:06.477
Updates should be tested. A new model file, changed configuration, updated client, or adjusted server setting can improve the workflow or break assumptions.

11
00:01:06.477 --> 00:01:11.186
Test important prompts and integrations before relying on the change.

12
00:01:11.186 --> 00:01:16.288
Sovereign AI can become important infrastructure for an individual or team.

13
00:01:16.288 --> 00:01:28.218
Dependable local AI needs basic care: ownership, notes, backups, version awareness, resource monitoring, safe shutdown, and a plan for troubleshooting when something changes.

14
00:01:28.218 --> 00:01:34.967
Operational discipline is what keeps a helpful local tool from becoming a fragile personal shortcut.

15
00:01:34.967 --> 00:01:39.284
If people rely on the workflow, it deserves routine maintenance.
